TEXT(beginproblem());

############################################################
# PG Setup Code
############################################################
Context("ImplicitPlane");
Context()->variables->are(x=>'Real');
$solstring = "x+1=6";
$solution = ImplicitPlane($solstring);
$evaluator = $solution->cmp(
  cmp_class=>'a linear equation',
  correct_ans=>$solstring,
  correct_ans_latex_string=>$solstring,
  checker => sub {
    my ($correct,$student,$ansHash) = @_;
    ($left, $right) = split('=',$ansHash->{original_student_ans});
    $left = Formula($left);
    $right= Formula($right);
    $ansHash->{preview_latex_string} = $ansHash->{original_student_ans};
    $ansHash->{preview_text_string} = $ansHash->{original_student_ans};
    $ansHash->{student_ans} = $ansHash->{original_student_ans};
    Value::Error('Your left side is just x') if ($left == Formula('x'));
    Value::Error('Your right side is just x') if ($right == Formula('x'));
    return ($correct == $student);
  }
);

############################################################
# Body
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML
Write a linear equation whose solution is [`x = 5\text{.}`] You may not write an equation whose left side is just "[`x`]" or whose right side is just "[`x`]".

There are infinitely many correct answers to this problem. _Be creative._ After finding an equation that works, see if you can come up with a different one that also works.

[_]{$evaluator}{20}

END_PGML

############################################################
# End Problem
############################################################

ENDDOCUMENT();

statement.p:
Fill in the blanks with the numbers 48 and 85 (using each number only once) to create an equation where x has the greatest possible value.




 {}+x={} 




 {}={} {}\cdot x

webwork-reps.pg:
############################################# ### Generated from PreTeXt source ### on 2024-08-11T14:52:00-07:00 ### A recent stable commit (2022-07-01): ### 6c761d3dba23af92cba35001c852aac04ae99a5f ### ### https://pretextbook.org ### ############################################# ## DBsubject() ## DBchapter() ## DBsection() ## Level() ## KEYWORDS() ## TitleText1() ## EditionText1() ## AuthorText1() ## Section1(not reported) ## Problem1(1.5.6.72) ## Author() ## Institution() ## Language(en-US)
DOCUMENT();
############################################################ # Load Macros ############################################################ loadMacros( "PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "AnswerFormatHelp.pl", "PGcourse.pl", ); COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt');
############################################################ # Header ############################################################ TEXT(beginproblem());
############################################################ # PG Setup Code ############################################################ Context('Numeric'); $little = random(10,50,2); $big = random(53,99,2);
############################################################ # Body ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML Fill in the blanks with the numbers [$little] and [$big] (using each number only once) to create an equation where [`x`] has the greatest possible value. a. [_]{$little}{3} [`{}+x={}`] [_]{$big}{3}
a. [_]{$big}{3} [`{}={}`] [_]{$little}{3}[`{}\cdot x`]
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION a. Consider which is greater: [`[$big] - [$little]`] or [`[$little] - [$big]\text{?}`] To make the largest value for [`x\text{,}`] you must put [$little] in the left blank and [$big] in the right blank because you solve this equation by subtracting [$little] from both sides. This makes [`x = [$big] - [$little]\text{,}`] which is the largest possible value for [`x\text{.}`]
a. Consider which is larger: [`\frac{[$big]}{[$little]}`] or [`\frac{[$little]}{[$big]}\text{?}`] To make the largest value for [`x\text{,}`] you must put [$big] in the left blank and [$little] in the right blank because you solve this equation by dividing both sides by [$little]. This makes [`x = \frac{[$big]}{[$little]}\text{,}`] which is the largest possible value for [`x\text{.}`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
############################################################ # End Problem ############################################################
ENDDOCUMENT();

