############################################################ # Header ############################################################ TEXT(beginproblem());
############################################################ # PG Setup Code ############################################################ Context("LinearRelation")->variables->are(t => 'Real'); $equation = LinearRelation("6.934 - 0.046t = 5");
############################################################ # Body ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML In the year 2020, the population of Bulgaria was [`6.934`] million. And it was declining at a rate of about [`0.046\,\frac{\text{million}}{\text{year}}\text{.}`] At this rate, how many years from 2020 will it take for Bulgaria's population to reach [`5`] million? Set up an equation where the solution is the number of years it will take.
[@KeyboardInstructions('Use [|t|]* for the variable.')@]**
[_]{$equation}{20}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION Since the solution will represent an amount of time, we choose to use [`t`] as the variable. Let [`t`] be the number of years from 2020 until Bulgaria's population reaches 5 million.
Now using the pattern for rate modeling from (1.8.1), we have:
[```\newcommand{\amp}{&} \begin{aligned} (\text{initial value}) - (\text{rate})\cdot\text{variable} \amp= (\text{final value})\\ 6.934 - 0.046t \amp= 5 \end{aligned} ```]
Notice that because the population is reducing over time, we used subtraction. In effect, the rate of "growth" is negative. We are not asked to solve this equation, just to write it down.
END_PGML_SOLUTION
############################################################ # End Problem ############################################################
ENDDOCUMENT();

