############################################################ # Load Macros ############################################################ loadMacros( "PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "scaffold.pl", "AnswerFormatHelp.pl", "parserNumberWithUnits.pl", "contextForm.pl", "contextFraction.pl", "randomPerson.pl", "parserLinearRelation.pl", "PGcourse.pl", ); COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt'); COMMENT('This problem is scaffolded with multiple parts');
############################################################ # Header ############################################################ TEXT(beginproblem());
############################################################ # PG Setup Code ############################################################ $p = randomPerson(); $b = random(12,20); $m = random(0.04, 0.05, 0.002); $sol = Real(($b - 1)/$m); $distance = NumberWithUnits($sol, 'mi'); $start = NumberWithUnits($b, 'gal'); $rate = NumberWithUnits($m, 'gal/mi'); Context("Interval"); Context()->flags->set(ignoreEndpointTypes => 1); $interval = Interval("(0,$sol]"); Context("LinearRelation")->variables->are(x => 'Real'); Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y'); $relation = Formula("$b - $m x >= 1"); Context("Fraction")->variables->are(x => 'Real'); Context()->flags->set(reduceConstants => 0, reduceFractions => 0); Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y', '(-x)/y', 'x/(-y)'); $solleft[0] = Formula("$b - $m x - $b")->reduce; $solright[0] = Formula("x - $b")->reduce; $solright[0] = $solright[0]->substitute(x => Formula("1")); $solleft[1] = Formula("-$m x")->reduce; $solright[1] = Formula("1 - $b")->reduce; $solleft[2] = Formula("(-$m x)/(-$m)"); $solright[2] = Formula("($solright[1])/(-$m)");
############################################################ # Body ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML [$p] is driving on the highway, and presently has [$start] of gasoline in [$p->their] tank. [$p->Their] car, _under ideal conditions_, uses gas at a rate of [$rate]. When the tank reaches only one gallon of gas, the low gas light will turn on and [$p] will start looking for a gas station. How far will [$p->they] drive before this happens?
END_PGML
############################################################ # Scaffold ############################################################ Scaffold::Begin(numbered => 1,is_open => "correct_or_first_incorrect",can_open => always,);
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Write an inequality to represent this situation, using [`x`] to represent how many miles [$p] might drive before the low gas light turns on.
[_]{$relation}{16}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION This is a rate exercise, and we can use
[``` (\text{initial value}) \pm (\text{rate})\cdot\text{variable} \mathrel{?} (\text{final value}) ```]
The question mark in the above is not an equal sign, because we are trying to set up an inequality. The initial value for the gas in [$p->their] tank is [`[$b]\text{,}`] [$p] is losing gas (so we will subtract, not add), the rate is [`[$m]\text{,}`] and the gas amount we are interested in reaching is [`1\text{.}`] We do not know far [$p] can drive until the gas gets that low, and we decide to use [`x`] to represent that distance. The rate of gas consumption we are using is only valid under ideal conditions. So the quantity [`[$b] - [$m] x`] will be _greater_ than or equal to the actual amount of gas left in the tank after driving [`x`] miles. And we want this to reach [`1\text{.}`] So the inequality we set up is:
[``` [$relation] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Solve this inequality. At most how far will [$p] drive before the low gas light turns on?
[_]{$distance}{16}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
[``` \begin{gathered} [$solleft[0]] \geq [$solright[0]]\\ [$solleft[1]] \geq [$solright[1]]\\ [$solleft[2]] \leq [$solright[2]]\\ x \leq [$sol] \end{gathered} ```]
So [$p] can drive at most [`[$sol]`] miles.
END_PGML_SOLUTION
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Use interval notation to express the number of miles [$p] might drive before the low gas light turns on.
[_]{$interval}{16}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION [$p] can drive at most [`[$sol]`] miles, so the right end of the interval notation for this interval will be [`[$sol]\text{,}`] with a bracket. We know [$p] has more than 1 gallon of gas when this all started, so [$p->they] can drive at least some positive number of miles. So the interval notation, accounting for the context of this exercise, is [`[$interval]\text{.}`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
Section::End();
Scaffold::End();
